## OOP Concepts

### Classes and Objects

**Definition**: A class is a blueprint for creating objects. An object is an instance of a class that has specific state and behavior.

**Theory of Classes**:
- **Encapsulation**: Bundling data and methods together
- **State**: Instance variables that represent object's data
- **Behavior**: Methods that operate on the state

```java
public class Car {
    // Instance variables (state of object)
    private String brand;
    private int year;
    private double price;
    
    // Constructor - initializes object when created
    public Car(String brand, int year) {
        this.brand = brand;
        this.year = year;
    }
    
    // Default constructor (if no constructor exists, compiler creates one)
    public Car() {
        this("Unknown", 0);
    }
    
    // Methods (behavior of object)
    public void drive() {
        System.out.println(brand + " is driving");
    }
    
    // Getter - provides controlled access to private variable
    public String getBrand() {
        return brand;
    }
    
    // Setter - provides controlled modification of private variable
    public void setBrand(String brand) {
        this.brand = brand;  // Validation can be added here
    }
    
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Car{" + "brand='" + brand + '\'' + ", year=" + year + '}';
    }
}

// Object Creation and Usage
Car car = new Car("Toyota", 2022);  // Object instantiation
car.drive();                        // Call method - "Toyota is driving"
System.out.println(car.getBrand()); // "Toyota"
```

**Key Points**:
- Classes are templates, objects are instances
- Instance variables unique to each object
- `this` keyword refers to current object
- Constructors initialize object state
- Getters/Setters provide encapsulation

### Inheritance

**Definition**: Inheritance is a mechanism where a derived class (child/subclass) inherits properties and methods from a base class (parent/superclass). It promotes code reuse and establishes "IS-A" relationship.

**Theory of Inheritance**:
- **Code Reuse**: Child class inherits parent's methods
- **Hierarchical Relationship**: Establishes class hierarchy
- **Method Overriding**: Child can override parent's methods
- **IS-A Relationship**: Child IS-A type of parent

**Inheritance Types**:
1. **Single Inheritance**: One parent (supported)
2. **Multi-level Inheritance**: Grandparent → Parent → Child (supported)
3. **Multiple Inheritance**: Multiple parents (not supported in classes, use interfaces)
4. **Hierarchical Inheritance**: One parent, multiple children (supported)

```java
// Parent class (Superclass)
public class Animal {
    protected String name;  // Protected - accessible to subclasses
    protected int age;
    
    public Animal(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
    
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating");
    }
    
    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println(name + " is sleeping");
    }
}

// Child class (Subclass)
public class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;
    
    public Dog(String name, int age, String breed) {
        super(name, age);  // Call parent constructor
        this.breed = breed;
    }
    
    // Method Overriding - redefine parent's method
    @Override
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating dog food");
    }
    
    // New method specific to Dog
    public void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " barks loudly");
    }
    
    // Method addition
    public String getBreed() {
        return breed;
    }
}

// Another Child class
public class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating cat food");
    }
    
    public void meow() {
        System.out.println(name + " meows");
    }
}

// Usage
Dog dog = new Dog("Buddy", 5, "Labrador");
dog.eat();      // Buddy is eating dog food (overridden method)
dog.sleep();    // Buddy is sleeping (inherited method)
dog.bark();     // Buddy barks loudly (new method)

Cat cat = new Cat("Whiskers", 3);
cat.eat();      // Whiskers is eating cat food
cat.meow();     // Whiskers meows
```

**super vs this**:
```java
public class Parent {
    int value = 10;
}

public class Child extends Parent {
    int value = 20;
    
    public void display() {
        System.out.println(this.value);     // 20 (child's value)
        System.out.println(super.value);    // 10 (parent's value)
    }
}
```

**Key Points**:
- `extends` keyword establishes inheritance
- `super` accesses parent class methods/constructors
- `@Override` annotation indicates method overriding
- Protected keyword allows access to subclasses
- Is-A relationship represents inheritance

### Polymorphism

**Definition**: Polymorphism means "many forms". It allows objects to take multiple forms or methods to behave differently based on context.

**Types of Polymorphism**:

**1. Method Overriding (Runtime/Dynamic Polymorphism)**:
```java
public class Animal {
    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Cat meows");
    }
}

// Polymorphic behavior - same reference, different behavior
Animal animal1 = new Dog();
Animal animal2 = new Cat();
Animal animal3 = new Animal();

animal1.sound();  // "Dog barks" (determined at runtime)
animal2.sound();  // "Cat meows"
animal3.sound();  // "Animal makes a sound"

// Without polymorphism, we'd need:
if (animal1 instanceof Dog) {
    ((Dog) animal1).sound();
}
```

**Theory of Method Overriding**:
- Method resolution happens at runtime (runtime binding)
- Based on actual object type, not reference type
- Enables writing flexible and extensible code
- Follows "Program to Interface" principle

**2. Method Overloading (Compile-time/Static Polymorphism)**:
```java
public class Calculator {
    // Overloading by parameter type
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    public double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    // Overloading by number of parameters
    public int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
    
    // Overloading by parameter order
    public void display(int a, String b) {
        System.out.println("Int: " + a + ", String: " + b);
    }
    
    public void display(String a, int b) {
        System.out.println("String: " + a + ", Int: " + b);
    }
}

// Method resolution at compile-time
Calculator calc = new Calculator();
System.out.println(calc.add(5, 10));           // 15 (int version)
System.out.println(calc.add(5.5, 10.5));       // 16.0 (double version)
System.out.println(calc.add(5, 10, 15));       // 30 (three params)

calc.display(5, "Hello");   // Int: 5, String: Hello
calc.display("Hello", 5);   // String: Hello, Int: 5
```

**Method Overloading Rules**:
- Method name must be same
- Parameters must differ in:
  - Type
  - Number
  - Order
- Return type alone cannot overload
- Can have different access modifiers

**Overriding vs Overloading**:
| Feature | Overriding | Overloading |
|---------|-----------|------------|
| Definition | Same method in parent and child | Multiple methods with same name |
| Resolution | Runtime (Dynamic binding) | Compile-time (Static binding) |
| Inheritance | Requires inheritance | Not required |
| Parameters | Must be same | Must differ |
| Return type | Can be covariant | Can be different |
| Purpose | Runtime polymorphism | Compile-time polymorphism |

**Key Points**:
- Overriding enables behavioral polymorphism
- Overloading enables compile-time flexibility
- `instanceof` checks actual type before casting
- Polymorphism reduces code coupling

### Encapsulation

**Definition**: Encapsulation is bundling data (variables) and methods together while hiding internal details. It uses access modifiers to control access.

**Theory of Encapsulation**:
- **Data Hiding**: Internal state hidden from outside world
- **Controlled Access**: Access through public getter/setter methods
- **Validation**: Can validate data before modification
- **Maintainability**: Internal implementation can change without affecting external code
- **Security**: Prevents unauthorized or incorrect data modification

**Levels of Access Control**:
1. **Public**: Accessible everywhere
2. **Protected**: Accessible within package and subclasses
3. **Default (Package-private)**: Accessible only within same package
4. **Private**: Accessible only within the class

```java
public class BankAccount {
    // Private - completely hidden from outside
    private double balance;
    private String accountNumber;
    private String accountHolder;
    
    // Constructor
    public BankAccount(String accountNumber, String accountHolder, double initialBalance) {
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
        this.accountHolder = accountHolder;
        this.balance = initialBalance;
    }
    
    // Public getter - read-only access to balance
    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
    
    // Public getter
    public String getAccountNumber() {
        return accountNumber;
    }
    
    // Public methods with validation
    public void deposit(double amount) {
        // Validation: prevent negative deposits
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Deposit amount must be positive");
        }
        balance += amount;
        System.out.println("Deposited: " + amount + ", New Balance: " + balance);
    }
    
    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        // Validation: prevent negative withdrawals and overdraft
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Withdrawal amount must be positive");
        }
        if (amount > balance) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Insufficient funds");
        }
        balance -= amount;
        System.out.println("Withdrew: " + amount + ", New Balance: " + balance);
    }
    
    // Can be called within class only
    private void logTransaction(String type, double amount) {
        System.out.println("[LOG] " + type + ": " + amount);
    }
}

// Usage
BankAccount account = new BankAccount("ACC001", "John Doe", 1000);
System.out.println(account.getBalance());  // 1000
account.deposit(500);                      // Deposited: 500, New Balance: 1500
account.withdraw(200);                     // Withdrew: 200, New Balance: 1300

// account.balance = -500; // ERROR: cannot access private field
// account.logTransaction("hack", 1000); // ERROR: private method
```

**Benefits of Encapsulation**:
```java
// Example: Change internal implementation without affecting users
public class Circle {
    private double radius;
    
    // If we store as diameter internally instead
    private double diameter;  // Change implementation
    
    public void setRadius(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
        this.diameter = radius * 2;  // Update diameter
    }
    
    public double getRadius() {
        return radius;  // External code still works
    }
    
    public double getArea() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}
// Users don't know about diameter, so they're not affected by change
```

**Key Points**:
- Always use private for data fields
- Provide public getters/setters only when needed
- Setters should validate data
- Encapsulation = Data Hiding + Controlled Access
- Enables maintainability and flexibility

### Abstraction

**Definition**: Abstraction is hiding complex implementation details and showing only essential features. It focuses on "What" not "How".

**Theory of Abstraction**:
- **Simplification**: Hide complexity, show simple interface
- **Focus on Interface**: Define what object does, not how
- **Implementation Independence**: Change implementation without affecting users
- **Reduction of Complexity**: Users work with abstract concept
- **Code Maintainability**: Easier to modify internal logic

**Abstraction through Abstract Classes**:
```java
// Abstract class - cannot be instantiated
public abstract class Shape {
    protected String color;
    
    public Shape(String color) {
        this.color = color;
    }
    
    // Abstract methods - no implementation
    abstract void draw();
    abstract double area();
    
    // Concrete method - has implementation
    public void info() {
        System.out.println("This is a shape with color: " + color);
    }
    
    public void fill() {
        System.out.println("Filling with color: " + color);
    }
}

// Concrete class - implements abstract methods
public class Circle extends Shape {
    private double radius;
    
    public Circle(String color, double radius) {
        super(color);
        this.radius = radius;
    }
    
    @Override
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing circle with radius: " + radius);
    }
    
    @Override
    double area() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

public class Rectangle extends Shape {
    private double width, height;
    
    public Rectangle(String color, double width, double height) {
        super(color);
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }
    
    @Override
    void draw() {
        System.out.println("Drawing rectangle: " + width + "x" + height);
    }
    
    @Override
    double area() {
        return width * height;
    }
}

// Usage
List<Shape> shapes = new ArrayList<>();
shapes.add(new Circle("Red", 5));
shapes.add(new Rectangle("Blue", 4, 6));

for (Shape shape : shapes) {
    shape.draw();           // Calls respective draw method
    System.out.println("Area: " + shape.area());
    shape.info();           // Inherited concrete method
}

// Shape shape = new Shape("Green"); // ERROR: cannot instantiate abstract class
```

**Abstraction through Interfaces**:
```java
// Interface - contracts/agreements
public interface Animal {
    // Abstract methods (public, no implementation)
    void eat();
    void sleep();
    void makeSound();
    
    // Concrete method (Java 8+)
    default void breathe() {
        System.out.println("Animal is breathing");
    }
    
    // Static method
    static void info() {
        System.out.println("Animals are living organisms");
    }
}

// Implementation
public class Dog implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Dog eats dog food");
    }
    
    @Override
    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Dog sleeps");
    }
    
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

public class Bird implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void eat() {
        System.out.println("Bird eats seeds");
    }
    
    @Override
    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println("Bird sleeps on branch");
    }
    
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Bird chirps");
    }
}

// Usage
Animal dog = new Dog();
Animal bird = new Bird();

dog.eat();           // "Dog eats dog food"
dog.breathe();       // "Animal is breathing"
bird.makeSound();    // "Bird chirps"
```

**Abstract Class vs Interface**:
| Feature | Abstract Class | Interface |
|---------|---|---|
| Multiple inheritance | No (extends) | Yes (implements multiple) |
| Constructor | Yes | No |
| Methods | Abstract + Concrete | Abstract only (Java 8+ default) |
| Variables | Any type | Constants (public static final) |
| Access Modifiers | Any | Public |
| Purpose | Share code among related classes | Define contract/behavior |
| Instantiation | Cannot instantiate | Cannot instantiate |

**Key Points**:
- Abstract classes for related classes sharing code
- Interfaces for unrelated classes defining behavior
- Use abstraction to reduce complexity
- Focus on "what" not "how"
- Hide implementation details from users

### Access Modifiers

**Definition**: Access modifiers control the visibility and accessibility of classes, methods, and variables.

**Four Levels of Access Control**:

```java
// public - accessible everywhere
public class PublicClass {
    public int publicVar = 1;
    public void publicMethod() { }
}

// protected - accessible in same package and by subclasses
protected class ProtectedClass {
    protected int protectedVar = 2;
    protected void protectedMethod() { }
}

// default/package-private - accessible only in same package (no modifier)
class DefaultClass {
    int defaultVar = 3;
    void defaultMethod() { }
}

// private - accessible only within the class
public class PrivateExample {
    private int privateVar = 4;
    private void privateMethod() { }
    
    public void accessPrivate() {
        // Can access within same class
        System.out.println(privateVar);
        privateMethod();
    }
}
```

**Access Modifier Matrix**:
| Modifier | Class | Package | Subclass | World |
|----------|-------|---------|----------|-------|
| public | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| protected | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ |
| default | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ |
| private | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ |

**Best Practices**:
```java
public class BestPractices {
    // Private for data hiding
    private String internalData;
    
    // Public for interface
    public String getData() {
        return internalData;
    }
    
    public void setData(String data) {
        if (data != null && !data.isEmpty()) {
            this.internalData = data;
        }
