    }
    
    // Private for internal use
    private void helperMethod() {
        
    }
}
```

**Key Points**:
- Start with private, expand if needed
- Use public only for API methods
- Protected for subclass access
- Private for encapsulation

### Static Members

**Definition**: Static members belong to the class itself, not to any specific instance. They are shared across all instances of the class.

**Theory of Static Members**:
- **Class-level**: Belong to class, not instances
- **Shared State**: One copy for all instances
- **Memory Efficiency**: No duplication across instances
- **Access**: Can be accessed via class name or instance
- **Initialization**: Initialized when class loads (before any instance creation)

```java
public class Counter {
    // Static variable - shared across all instances
    static int count = 0;
    
    // Instance variable - unique for each instance
    int instanceValue;
    
    public Counter() {
        count++;  // Increments shared variable
    }
    
    // Static method - can access only static members
    static void printCount() {
        System.out.println("Count: " + count);
        // System.out.println(instanceValue); // ERROR: cannot access instance variable
    }
    
    // Instance method - can access both static and instance members
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Count: " + count);
        System.out.println("Instance Value: " + instanceValue);
    }
}

// Usage
Counter c1 = new Counter();
Counter c2 = new Counter();
Counter c3 = new Counter();

Counter.printCount();  // Count: 3 (shared among all instances)
System.out.println(c1.count); // 3 (accessing via instance also works)

c1.display();  // Shows shared count value
```

**Static Variables**:
```java
public class Configuration {
    // Static constant - convention: UPPER_CASE
    static final double PI = 3.14159;
    
    // Static variable
    static String appName = "MyApp";
    static int version = 1;
    
    // Instance variable
    String userId;
    
    public static void printConfig() {
        System.out.println("App: " + appName + " v" + version);
        System.out.println("Pi: " + PI);
    }
}

Configuration.printConfig();  // App: MyApp v1
// Pi: 3.14159
```

**Static Initialization Block**:
```java
public class Initialization {
    static int value;
    static String message;
    
    // Static block - executes when class is loaded (only once)
    static {
        value = 100;
        message = "Static block executed";
        System.out.println("Class initialized");
        
        // Read configuration
        // Connect to database
        // Load resources
    }
    
    // Instance initialization block - executes before constructor
    {
        System.out.println("Instance block executed");
    }
    
    public Initialization() {
        System.out.println("Constructor executed");
    }
}

// Output when Initialization is first referenced:
// Class initialized
// Then for each new instance:
// Instance block executed
// Constructor executed
```

**Static Methods**:
```java
public class MathUtility {
    // Static method - utility methods don't need instance
    public static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    
    public static double sqrt(double number) {
        return Math.sqrt(number);
    }
    
    public static boolean isPrime(int number) {
        if (number <= 1) return false;
        for (int i = 2; i < number; i++) {
            if (number % i == 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
}

// Usage - no instance needed
int sum = MathUtility.add(5, 10);  // 15
boolean prime = MathUtility.isPrime(7);  // true
```

**Static vs Instance Members**:
| Feature | Static | Instance |
|---------|--------|----------|
| Belongs to | Class | Object |
| Copies | One | One per instance |
| Memory | Shared | Individual |
| Access | ClassName.member | instance.member |
| Creation time | Class load time | Object creation |
| Default value | Same as instance | Same as instance |

**Common Use Cases for Static**:
```java
public class Logger {
    // Static methods for logging
    public static void info(String message) {
        System.out.println("[INFO] " + message);
    }
    
    public static void error(String message) {
        System.err.println("[ERROR] " + message);
    }
}

public class Constants {
    // Static constants
    public static final double PI = 3.14159;
    public static final int MAX_SIZE = 100;
    public static final String APP_NAME = "MyApplication";
}

public class Singleton {
    // Static instance for singleton pattern
    private static Singleton instance;
    
    private Singleton() { }
    
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

**Key Points**:
- Static members are loaded once when class loads
- Shared across all instances (memory efficient)
- Static methods cannot access instance variables
- Static blocks execute in order of definition
- Use static for utility methods and constants
- Avoid excessive static usage (less flexible)

### Final Keyword

**Definition**: The `final` keyword restricts modification. It can be applied to classes, methods, and variables.

**Theory of Final Keyword**:
- **Immutability**: Prevents change or override
- **Security**: Prevents unauthorized modification
- **Performance**: Compiler can optimize final members
- **Design Intent**: Shows what shouldn't be changed

```java
// Final Class - cannot be extended
public final class ImmutableClass {
    // Prevents subclassing - ensures implementation cannot be changed
    public void doSomething() {
    }
}

// public class SubClass extends ImmutableClass { } // ERROR: Cannot extend final class

// Examples of final classes in Java
// String - final (immutable)
// Integer, Long, Double - final
// LocalDate - final (immutable)
```

**Final Methods** - cannot be overridden:
```java
public class Parent {
    // Final method - subclasses cannot override
    public final void importantMethod() {
        System.out.println("This implementation is critical");
        System.out.println("Cannot be changed by subclasses");
    }
    
    public void normalMethod() {
        System.out.println("Can be overridden");
    }
}

public class Child extends Parent {
    @Override
    public void normalMethod() {
        System.out.println("Overridden in child");
    }
    
    // @Override
    // public void importantMethod() { } // ERROR: Cannot override final method
}
```

**Final Variables** - cannot be reassigned:
```java
public class Constants {
    // Final instance variable - must be initialized
    private final int MAX_SIZE = 100;
    
    // Final static variable - constant
    public static final double PI = 3.14159;
    public static final String APP_NAME = "MyApp";
    
    // Final local variable
    public void method() {
        final int value = 10;
        // value = 20; // ERROR: Cannot reassign final variable
    }
}
```

**Final with Collections**:
```java
public class FinalCollections {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Final reference - cannot change reference
        final List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        
        // But can modify contents (reference is final, not object)
        list.add("Hello");
        list.add("World");
        
        // list = new ArrayList<>(); // ERROR: Cannot reassign final reference
        
        System.out.println(list);  // [Hello, World]
    }
}
```

**Use Cases for Final**:
```java
// 1. Constants
public class Constants {
    public static final int MAX_RETRIES = 3;
    public static final long TIMEOUT_MS = 5000;
}

// 2. Security - prevent extension
public final class SecurityCriticalClass {
}

// 3. Immutability
public final class ImmutablePoint {
    private final int x;
    private final int y;
    
    public ImmutablePoint(int x, int y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }
    
    public int getX() { return x; }
    public int getY() { return y; }
}

// 4. Performance optimization (compiler)
public class Optimization {
    public static final boolean DEBUG = false;
    
    public void method() {
        if (DEBUG) {
            // Compiler can remove this entire block
            System.out.println("Debug mode");
        }
    }
}
```

**Benefits of Final**:
- **Clarity**: Shows what shouldn't be modified
- **Security**: Prevents unauthorized changes
- **Performance**: Compiler optimizations
- **Thread-safety**: Final variables are inherently thread-safe
- **Design**: Communicates design intent

**Key Points**:
- Final class cannot be extended
- Final method cannot be overridden
- Final variable cannot be reassigned
- All declarations in final class are implicitly final-friendly
- Final reference ≠ immutable object

