## Collections Framework

**Definition**: The Collections Framework provides unified architecture for storing and manipulating groups of objects. It includes interfaces, implementations, and algorithms.

**Collections Hierarchy**:
```
Collection (Interface)
├── List (Ordered, allows duplicates)
│   ├── ArrayList (resizable array)
│   ├── LinkedList (doubly-linked list)
│   └── Vector (synchronized, legacy)
├── Set (Unique elements, unordered)
│   ├── HashSet (unordered, O(1) ops)
│   ├── LinkedHashSet (insertion order)
│   └── TreeSet (sorted order, O(log n) ops)
└── Queue (FIFO by default)
    ├── LinkedList (FIFO)
    └── PriorityQueue (ordered by priority)

Map (Separate hierarchy, key-value pairs)
├── HashMap (unordered, O(1) average)
├── LinkedHashMap (insertion order)
├── TreeMap (sorted by key, O(log n))
└── Hashtable (synchronized, legacy)
```

**Key Concepts**:
- **Ordering**: Maintains element sequence
- **Performance**: Time complexity for operations
- **Uniqueness**: Allows or rejects duplicates
- **Synchronization**: Thread-safe or not
- **Null Handling**: Allows or rejects null

### List Interface

**Definition**: List is an ordered collection that allows duplicate elements. Elements have indices (position-based access).

```java
import java.util.*;

// ArrayList - resizable dynamic array
// Characteristics: O(1) random access, O(n) insertion/deletion
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("Apple");
list.add("Banana");
list.add("Cherry");
list.add(1, "Blueberry");        // Insert at index 1

// Access elements
System.out.println(list.get(0)); // "Apple"
System.out.println(list.size()); // 4

// Modify elements
list.set(0, "Apricot");          // Replace element at index 0

// Remove elements
list.remove(1);                  // Remove element at index 1
list.remove("Cherry");           // Remove specific element

// Iteration
for (String fruit : list) {
    System.out.println(fruit);
}

// Stream operations (Java 8+)
list.stream()
    .filter(s -> s.startsWith("A"))
    .forEach(System.out::println);

// LinkedList - doubly-linked list
// Characteristics: O(n) random access, O(1) insertion/deletion at ends
LinkedList<Integer> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();
linkedList.add(1);
linkedList.addFirst(0);          // Add at beginning
linkedList.addLast(2);           // Add at end
linkedList.add(1, 5);            // Insert at index 1

System.out.println(linkedList.getFirst());  // 0
System.out.println(linkedList.getLast());   // 2
linkedList.removeFirst();        // Remove first element
linkedList.removeLast();         // Remove last element

// As Queue (FIFO)
linkedList.offer(3);             // Add to end
linkedList.poll();               // Remove from front

// Vector - synchronized ArrayList (legacy, slower)
Vector<String> vector = new Vector<>();
vector.add("Element");

// Stack - extends Vector, LIFO behavior
Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
stack.push(1);
stack.push(2);
stack.push(3);
System.out.println(stack.peek()); // 3 (top without removing)
System.out.println(stack.pop());  // 3 (remove and return)
System.out.println(stack.search(1)); // 3 (position from top)
```

**ArrayList vs LinkedList**:
| Operation | ArrayList | LinkedList |
|-----------|-----------|-----------|
| Access (get) | O(1) | O(n) |
| Insert (middle) | O(n) | O(1) |
| Insert (end) | O(1) amortized | O(1) |
| Remove | O(n) | O(n) |
| Memory | Compact | Extra pointers |
| Use Case | Frequent access | Frequent insert/delete at ends |

**Key Points for Lists**:
- Use ArrayList for random access
- Use LinkedList for frequent insertions/deletions
- ArrayList wastes space when shrinking
- LinkedList wastes memory on pointers

### Set Interface

**Definition**: Set is a collection of unique elements (no duplicates). Order depends on implementation.

```java
// HashSet - unordered, unique elements
// Characteristics: O(1) average for add/remove/contains
Set<String> hashSet = new HashSet<>();
hashSet.add("Apple");
hashSet.add("Banana");
hashSet.add("Cherry");
hashSet.add("Apple");  // Duplicate - ignored
System.out.println(hashSet.size());  // 3

// Check if element exists
if (hashSet.contains("Banana")) {
    System.out.println("Found Banana");
}

// Remove element
hashSet.remove("Banana");

// Iteration (order not guaranteed)
for (String fruit : hashSet) {
    System.out.println(fruit);
}

// Stream operations
hashSet.stream()
       .filter(s -> s.length() > 5)
       .forEach(System.out::println);


// LinkedHashSet - maintains insertion order
// Characteristics: Insertion order preserved, O(1) operations
Set<String> linkedHashSet = new LinkedHashSet<>();
linkedHashSet.add("Apple");
linkedHashSet.add("Banana");
linkedHashSet.add("Cherry");
// Iteration: Apple, Banana, Cherry (insertion order)

// TreeSet - sorted order, natural ordering or custom comparator
// Characteristics: O(log n) for add/remove/contains, sorted
Set<Integer> treeSet = new TreeSet<>();
treeSet.add(5);
treeSet.add(2);
treeSet.add(8);
treeSet.add(1);
System.out.println(treeSet);  // [1, 2, 5, 8] (sorted)

// Reverse order sorting
Set<Integer> reverseSorted = new TreeSet<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
reverseSorted.add(5);
reverseSorted.add(2);
reverseSorted.add(8);
System.out.println(reverseSorted);  // [8, 5, 2] (reverse sorted)

// Custom sorting with TreeSet
Set<String> sortedStrings = new TreeSet<>((s1, s2) -> s2.compareTo(s1));
sortedStrings.add("Apple");
sortedStrings.add("Banana");
sortedStrings.add("Cherry");
System.out.println(sortedStrings);  // [Cherry, Banana, Apple]

// Set operations
Set<Integer> set1 = new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3));
Set<Integer> set2 = new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(2, 3, 4));

// Union
Set<Integer> union = new HashSet<>(set1);
union.addAll(set2);  // {1, 2, 3, 4}

// Intersection
Set<Integer> intersection = new HashSet<>(set1);
intersection.retainAll(set2);  // {2, 3}

// Difference
Set<Integer> difference = new HashSet<>(set1);
difference.removeAll(set2);  // {1}
```

**Set Implementations Comparison**:
| Feature | HashSet | LinkedHashSet | TreeSet |
|---------|---------|--------------|---------|
| Ordering | No | Insertion | Sorted |
| Performance | O(1) | O(1) | O(log n) |
| Null values | Yes | Yes | No |
| Thread-safe | No | No | No |
| Memory | Least | Middle | Most |
| Use Case | Fast lookup | Ordered access | Range queries |

**Key Points for Sets**:
- Use for unique elements
- HashSet fastest for lookup
- LinkedHashSet preserves insertion order
- TreeSet provides sorted iteration
- No duplicate values allowed

### Map Interface

**Definition**: Map stores key-value pairs where each key is unique. Keys map to values, providing fast retrieval by key.

```java
// HashMap - unordered key-value storage
// Characteristics: O(1) average for put/get/remove, unordered
Map<String, Integer> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
hashMap.put("Alice", 25);
hashMap.put("Bob", 30);
hashMap.put("Charlie", 28);

// Access values
System.out.println(hashMap.get("Alice"));      // 25
System.out.println(hashMap.getOrDefault("David", 0)); // 0

// Check existence
System.out.println(hashMap.containsKey("Alice"));      // true
System.out.println(hashMap.containsValue(25));         // true

// Modify values
hashMap.put("Alice", 26);  // Update value
hashMap.remove("Bob");

// Iterate over entries
for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : hashMap.entrySet()) {
    String key = entry.getKey();
    Integer value = entry.getValue();
    System.out.println(key + ": " + value);
}

// Iterate over keys
for (String key : hashMap.keySet()) {
    System.out.println(key + ": " + hashMap.get(key));
}

// Iterate over values
for (Integer age : hashMap.values()) {
    System.out.println(age);
}

// Stream operations
hashMap.entrySet().stream()
       .filter(e -> e.getValue() > 25)
       .forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.getKey() + ": " + e.getValue()));

// Compute operations
hashMap.compute("Alice", (k, v) -> v == null ? 0 : v + 1);  // 27
hashMap.computeIfPresent("Alice", (k, v) -> v + 2);        // 29
hashMap.computeIfAbsent("Eve", k -> 22);                    // 22 for Eve


// LinkedHashMap - maintains insertion order
// Characteristics: O(1) operations, insertion order preserved
Map<String, String> linkedHashMap = new LinkedHashMap<>();
linkedHashMap.put("Name", "John");
linkedHashMap.put("Age", "25");
linkedHashMap.put("City", "NYC");
// Iteration order: Name, Age, City (insertion order)

// Access order variant (LRU cache)
Map<String, String> accessOrderMap = new LinkedHashMap<String, String>(16, 0.75f, true) {
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry eldest) {
        return size() > 3;  // Keep only 3 entries (LRU cache)
    }
};


// TreeMap - sorted by key
// Characteristics: O(log n) operations, natural ordering or comparator
Map<Integer, String> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();
treeMap.put(3, "Third");
treeMap.put(1, "First");
treeMap.put(2, "Second");
// Sorted by key: 1, 2, 3

// Range operations
Map<Integer, String> subset = ((TreeMap<Integer, String>) treeMap).subMap(1, 3); // 1 to 2 (exclusive 3)
Map<Integer, String> head = ((TreeMap<Integer, String>) treeMap).headMap(2);  // Keys < 2
Map<Integer, String> tail = ((TreeMap<Integer, String>) treeMap).tailMap(2);  // Keys >= 2


// Hashtable - synchronized (legacy, slower)
// Characteristics: Thread-safe but slower, legacy
Hashtable<String, Integer> hashtable = new Hashtable<>();
hashtable.put("Key", 1);


// WeakHashMap - uses weak references to keys
Map<String, String> weakMap = new WeakHashMap<>();


// IdentityHashMap - uses reference equality instead of value equality
Map<String, Integer> identityMap = new IdentityHashMap<>();


// EnumMap - optimized for enum keys
enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE }
Map<Color, String> enumMap = new EnumMap<>(Color.class);
enumMap.put(Color.RED, "Red");
```

**Map Implementations Comparison**:
| Feature | HashMap | LinkedHashMap | TreeMap | Hashtable |
|---------|---------|---------------|---------|-----------|
| Ordering | No | Insertion | Sorted | No |
| Performance | O(1) | O(1) | O(log n) | O(1) |
| Thread-safe | No | No | No | Yes |
| Null key/value | Yes/Yes | Yes/Yes | No/No | No/No |
| Use Case | General | Ordered | Range queries | Legacy |

**Merge and PutIfAbsent**:
```java
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("count", 1);

// putIfAbsent
map.putIfAbsent("count", 5);    // 1 (not updated)
map.putIfAbsent("new", 5);      // 5 (inserted)

// merge
map.merge("count", 10, Integer::sum);  // 11 (1 + 10)
map.merge("total", 100, (oldVal, newVal) -> oldVal + newVal);  // 100 (new key)
```

**Key Points for Maps**:
- Use HashMap for general purpose key-value storage
- Use LinkedHashMap to maintain insertion order
- Use TreeMap for sorted iteration or range queries
- Use EnumMap for enum keys (most efficient)
- HashMap supports null key (max 1), TreeMap doesn't

### Queue and Deque

**Definition**: Queue is a collection for holding elements prior to processing, typically FIFO (First-In-First-Out). Deque (Double Ended Queue) allows insertion/removal at both ends.

```java
// Queue (FIFO - First In First Out)
// Characteristics: Elements processed in order they're added
Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.add(1);       // Add to end
queue.add(2);
queue.add(3);

System.out.println(queue.peek());   // 1 (view front without removing)
System.out.println(queue.poll());   // 1 (remove and return front)
System.out.println(queue.remove()); // 2 (throw exception if empty)

// Iteration
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    System.out.println(queue.poll());
}

// PriorityQueue - elements ordered by priority, not insertion order
// Characteristics: Min-heap by default (smallest element first)
Queue<Integer> priorityQueue = new PriorityQueue<>();
priorityQueue.add(5);
priorityQueue.add(2);
priorityQueue.add(8);
priorityQueue.add(1);

System.out.println(priorityQueue.poll());  // 1 (smallest)
System.out.println(priorityQueue.poll());  // 2
System.out.println(priorityQueue.poll());  // 5

// Custom comparator (max-heap)
Queue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> b - a);
maxHeap.add(5);
maxHeap.add(2);
maxHeap.add(8);

System.out.println(maxHeap.poll());  // 8 (largest)
System.out.println(maxHeap.poll());  // 5
System.out.println(maxHeap.poll());  // 2

// PriorityQueue with custom objects
class Task implements Comparable<Task> {
    String name;
    int priority;
    
    public Task(String name, int priority) {
        this.name = name;
        this.priority = priority;
    }
    
    @Override
    public int compareTo(Task other) {
        return this.priority - other.priority;  // Lower priority value = higher priority
    }
}

Queue<Task> taskQueue = new PriorityQueue<>();
taskQueue.add(new Task("Task1", 3));
taskQueue.add(new Task("Task2", 1));
taskQueue.add(new Task("Task3", 2));

while (!taskQueue.isEmpty()) {
    Task task = taskQueue.poll();
    System.out.println(task.name + " (priority: " + task.priority + ")");
}
// Output: Task2 (1), Task3 (2), Task1 (3)


// Deque (Double Ended Queue)
// Characteristics: Insert/remove from both ends
Deque<String> deque = new LinkedList<>();

// Add operations
deque.addFirst("A");
deque.addLast("B");
deque.addLast("C");

// Remove operations
System.out.println(deque.removeFirst());  // "A"
System.out.println(deque.removeLast());   // "C"

// Peek operations (without removing)
System.out.println(deque.getFirst());     // "B"
System.out.println(deque.getLast());      // "B"

// Using as Stack
Deque<Integer> stack = new LinkedList<>();
stack.push(1);      // Add to front
stack.push(2);
stack.pop();        // Remove from front

// Using as Queue
Deque<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
q.add(1);           // Add to end
q.removeFirst();    // Remove from front
```

**Queue Operations Comparison**:
| Method | Add | Remove | Peek/Get |
|--------|-----|--------|----------|
| Throw exception | add() | remove() | element() |
| Return false/null | offer() | poll() | peek() |

**Use Cases**:
```java
// Task processing
Queue<Task> taskQueue = new LinkedList<>();

// Priority-based processing
Queue<Patient> priorityQueue = new PriorityQueue<>((p1, p2) -> p1.severity - p2.severity);

// Undo/Redo functionality
Deque<String> undoStack = new LinkedList<>();

// Level-order traversal in trees
Queue<TreeNode> bfs = new LinkedList<>();
```

**Key Points for Queue**:
- FIFO for regular Queue
- PriorityQueue for priority-based processing
- Deque for both ends access
- Use poll() instead of remove() to avoid exceptions
- Time complexity: O(1) for add/remove/peek
System.out.println(queue.peek());   // 1 (view front)
System.out.println(queue.poll());   // 1 (remove front)
System.out.println(queue.size());   // 2

// Priority Queue (heap-based)
Queue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
pq.add(5);
pq.add(1);
pq.add(3);
while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    System.out.println(pq.poll());  // 1, 3, 5 (sorted order)
}

// Deque (Double Ended Queue)
Deque<Integer> deque = new LinkedList<>();
deque.addFirst(1);
deque.addLast(2);
deque.addLast(3);
System.out.println(deque.removeFirst());  // 1
System.out.println(deque.removeLast());   // 3
```

### Collections Utility Methods

```java
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6);

// Sorting
Collections.sort(list);
System.out.println(list);  // [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9]

// Reverse sorting
Collections.sort(list, Collections.reverseOrder());

// Searching (list must be sorted)
int index = Collections.binarySearch(list, 5);

// Min/Max
int min = Collections.min(list);
int max = Collections.max(list);

// Reverse
Collections.reverse(list);

// Shuffle
Collections.shuffle(list);

// Copy
List<Integer> copy = new ArrayList<>(list);

// Frequency
int count = Collections.frequency(list, 1);  // How many times 1 appears

