## Exception Handling

**Definition**: Exception handling is a mechanism to handle runtime errors, allowing program execution to continue or terminate gracefully.

**Why Exception Handling?**:
- **Graceful Degradation**: Program doesn't crash unexpectedly
- **Resource Management**: Ensures cleanup (files, connections)
- **Error Logging**: Record errors for debugging
- **User Experience**: Provide meaningful error messages
- **Robustness**: Handle expected error scenarios

### Try-Catch-Finally

```java
// Basic try-catch structure
try {
    // Code that might throw exception
    int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
    System.out.println(arr[5]);  // IndexOutOfBoundsException
} catch (IndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
    // Handle specific exception
    System.out.println("Index out of bounds: " + e.getMessage());
} catch (Exception e) {
    // Handle general exception (catch all)
    System.out.println("General exception: " + e.getMessage());
} finally {
    // Always executes - cleanup code
    System.out.println("Finally block always executes");
}

// Exception object methods
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println(e.getMessage());        // "/ by zero"
    System.out.println(e.getClass().getName()); // "java.lang.ArithmeticException"
    e.printStackTrace();                        // Print full stack trace
}

// Multiple catch blocks (specific to general)
try {
    // Risky code
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    // Handle file not found
} catch (IOException e) {
    // Handle IO errors (parent of FileNotFoundException)
} catch (Exception e) {
    // Catch all
}

// Multi-catch (Java 7+)
try {
    // Code
} catch (IOException | SQLException e) {
    // Handle multiple exception types
    System.out.println("IO or SQL error");
}

// Try-with-resources (Java 7+) - automatic resource closure
try (FileReader reader = new FileReader("file.txt")) {
    // Use reader
    int data = reader.read();
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.out.println("Error reading file");
}
// reader automatically closed even if exception occurs
```

**Theory - Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions**:

```
Checked Exceptions:
- Must be handled (try-catch) or declared (throws)
- Compiler enforces at compile time
- Examples: IOException, SQLException, FileNotFoundException
- Used for recoverable conditions

Unchecked Exceptions (RuntimeException):
- Optional to handle (up to programmer)
- Runtime exceptions
- Examples: NullPointerException, IndexOutOfBoundsException
- Used for programming errors
```

### Exception Hierarchy

```
Object
  └─ Throwable
      ├─ Error (system-level, not recoverable)
      │  ├─ OutOfMemoryError
      │  ├─ StackOverflowError
      │  └─ VirtualMachineError
      │
      └─ Exception
          ├─ Checked Exception (must handle)
          │  ├─ IOException (file operations)
          │  ├─ SQLException (database operations)
          │  ├─ FileNotFoundException
          │  ├─ ClassNotFoundException
          │  └─ InterruptedException
          │
          └─ RuntimeException (Unchecked)
             ├─ NullPointerException (null reference)
             ├─ ArithmeticException (divide by zero)
             ├─ ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
             ├─ ClassCastException (invalid cast)
             ├─ IllegalArgumentException
             ├─ NumberFormatException
             ├─ ConcurrentModificationException
             └─ UnsupportedOperationException
```

