### Synchronization

**Problem**: Without synchronization, race conditions occur when multiple threads access shared resources.

```java
// ✗ Without synchronization - Race condition
public class Counter {
    private int count = 0;  // Shared resource
    
    public void increment() {
        count++;  // NOT atomic: read, modify, write
    }
    
    public int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

// If two threads increment simultaneously:
// Thread 1: read(0), modify(1), write(1)
// Thread 2: read(0), modify(1), write(1)
// Result: 1 (should be 2) - RACE CONDITION


// ✓ With synchronized method
public class SynchronizedCounter {
    private int count = 0;
    
    // Lock on this object
    public synchronized void increment() {
        count++;  // Only one thread can execute at a time
    }
    
    public synchronized int getCount() {
        return count;
    }
}

// ✓ With synchronized block (more granular)
public class SynchronizedBlock {
    private int count = 0;
    
    public void increment() {
        synchronized(this) {  // Lock on this object
            count++;
        }
    }
    
    public void multipleOperations() {
        // Non-synchronized code
        int temp = someCalculation();
        
        synchronized(this) {
            // Only critical section is synchronized
            count += temp;
        }
        
        // More non-synchronized code
    }
}

// ✓ Using Lock interface (more flexible than synchronized)
import java.util.concurrent.locks.*;

public class LockExample {
    private int count = 0;
    private Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    
    public void increment() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            count++;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();  // Always unlock in finally
        }
    }
    
    public int getCount() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            return count;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
    
    // Trylock - non-blocking
    public boolean tryIncrement() {
        if (lock.tryLock()) {
            try {
                count++;
                return true;
            } finally {
                lock.unlock();
            }
        }
        return false;  // Could not acquire lock
    }
    
    // Fair lock - ensures waiting threads get lock in order
    private Lock fairLock = new ReentrantLock(true);
}

// ✓ Synchronized static method (locks on class)
public class StaticSync {
    private static int staticCount = 0;
    
    public static synchronized void increment() {
        staticCount++;  // Locked on StaticSync.class
    }
}

// ✓ Atomic classes (for simple operations)
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.*;

public class AtomicExample {
    private AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);
    
    public void increment() {
        count.incrementAndGet();  // Atomic operation
    }
    
    public int getCount() {
        return count.get();
    }
}
```

**synchronized vs Lock**:
| Feature | synchronized | Lock |
|---------|---|---|
| Syntax | Keyword | Interface |
| Flexibility | Fixed | Fine-grained control |
| Fair ordering | No (by default) | Yes (optional) |
| Try-lock | No | Yes (tryLock) |
| Timeout | No | Yes (tryLock with timeout) |
| Multiple conditions | No | Yes (Condition) |
| Performance | Good | Better with contention |

### Thread Pool (Executor Framework)

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

// Fixed thread pool - fixed number of threads
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

// Submit tasks
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    final int taskId = i;
    executor.submit(() -> {
        System.out.println("Task " + taskId + " executing");
        try {
            Thread.sleep(1000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
    });
}

// Shutdown executor
executor.shutdown();  // No new tasks, wait for existing
// executor.shutdownNow();  // Interrupt all tasks

// Wait for termination
if (!executor.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
    executor.shutdownNow();
}

// Cached thread pool - creates threads as needed
ExecutorService cachedPool = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

// Single thread executor - single thread processes tasks sequentially
ExecutorService singleExecutor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();

// Scheduled executor - execute tasks after delay or periodically
ScheduledExecutorService scheduler = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(2);

// Execute after 5 seconds
scheduler.schedule(() -> {
    System.out.println("Executed after delay");
}, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

// Execute every 10 seconds
scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> {
    System.out.println("Repeated task");
}, 0, 10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);

// Fork/Join pool - parallel processing
ForkJoinPool pool = ForkJoinPool.commonPool();

RecursiveTask<Integer> task = new RecursiveTask<Integer>() {
    protected Integer compute() {
        // Divide and conquer
        return 0;
    }
};

pool.invoke(task);
```

**Key Points**:
- Always use ExecutorService instead of creating threads manually
- Use synchronized or Lock for shared resource access
- Prefer Lock over synchronized for complex scenarios
- Use Atomic classes for simple counters
- Always shutdown ExecutorService properly
- Use try-finally or try-with-resources for cleanup
