## Comprehensive Java Concepts Summary

### Core OOP Principles (SOLID)

**1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)**
- Each class should have single reason to change
- Each class should have one responsibility

```java
// ✗ Violation: UserService doing too much
public class UserService {
    public void createUser(String name) { }
    public void saveToDatabase(User user) { }
    public void sendEmail(String email) { }
}

// ✓ Correct: Separate concerns
public class UserService {
    public void createUser(String name) { }
}

public class UserRepository {
    public void save(User user) { }
}

public class EmailService {
    public void send(String email) { }
}
```

**2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)**
- Open for extension, closed for modification
- Use inheritance and polymorphism

```java
// ✗ Violation: Must modify for each new shape
public class AreaCalculator {
    public double calculateArea(Shape shape) {
        if (shape instanceof Circle) return Math.PI * ((Circle)shape).radius * ((Circle)shape).radius;
        if (shape instanceof Rectangle) return ((Rectangle)shape).width * ((Rectangle)shape).height;
    }
}

// ✓ Correct: Extensible design
public abstract class Shape {
    public abstract double getArea();
}

public class Circle extends Shape {
    @Override
    public double getArea() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

public class AreaCalculator {
    public double calculateArea(Shape shape) {
        return shape.getArea();  // Works for any shape
    }
}
```

**3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)**
- Subclass can replace parent class without breaking code
- Contracts must be maintained

```java
// ✗ Violation
public class Bird {
    public void fly() { }
}

public class Penguin extends Bird {
    @Override
    public void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Penguins cannot fly");
    }
}

// ✓ Correct: Proper hierarchy
public interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

public class Eagle implements Flyable {
    @Override
    public void fly() { }
}

public class Penguin {
    // Doesn't implement Flyable
}
```

**4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)**
- Clients shouldn't depend on interfaces they don't use
- Create specific interfaces

```java
// ✗ Violation: Fat interface
public interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
}

// ✓ Correct: Segregated interfaces
public interface Workable {
    void work();
}

public interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}

public class Robot implements Workable {
    @Override
    public void work() { }
}

public class Human implements Workable, Eatable {
    @Override
    public void work() { }
    @Override
    public void eat() { }
}
```

**5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)**
- Depend on abstractions, not concrete classes
- High-level modules shouldn't depend on low-level modules

```java
// ✗ Violation: Tight coupling
public class PaymentProcessor {
    private CreditCard creditCard = new CreditCard();
    
    public void process() {
        creditCard.charge();
    }
}

// ✓ Correct: Dependency injection
public interface PaymentMethod {
    void charge();
}

public class PaymentProcessor {
    private PaymentMethod paymentMethod;
    
    public PaymentProcessor(PaymentMethod paymentMethod) {
        this.paymentMethod = paymentMethod;
    }
    
    public void process() {
        paymentMethod.charge();
    }
}
```

### Memory Management

**Stack Memory**:
- Stores primitive values and references
- Thread-private (each thread has own stack)
- Memory freed automatically when method returns
- LIFO (Last In First Out) structure
- Limited size (causes StackOverflowError if exceeded)

**Heap Memory**:
- Stores all objects
- Shared among all threads
- Memory freed by Garbage Collector
- Dynamically sized
- Slower access than stack

```java
void method() {
    int age = 25;           // Stack
    String name = "John";   // Reference on stack, object on heap
    Person person = new Person("Alice");  // Reference on stack, object on heap
    
    // When method returns:
    // - age, name, person references removed from stack
    // - Objects on heap eligible for GC if no other references
}
```

### String Pool Concept

```java
String s1 = "Hello";         // Created in String pool
String s2 = "Hello";         // References same object in pool
String s3 = new String("Hello");  // Created in heap

s1 == s2  // true (same reference)
s1 == s3  // false (different objects)
s1.equals(s3)  // true (same value)

// Intern method - add to pool or get from pool
String s4 = new String("Hello").intern();  // Gets from pool
s1 == s4  // true (same reference)
```

### Garbage Collection

**When GC runs**:
- When heap is full
- System memory is low
- Periodically in background
- JVM decides when (except System.gc() hint)

**How to help GC**:
```java
// Remove references to allow GC
MyClass obj = new MyClass();
// use obj
obj = null;  // Now eligible for GC

// Or use try-with-resources
try (Resource resource = new Resource()) {
    // Use resource
}  // Resource closed and eligible for GC
```

### Performance Optimization Tips

**1. Choose Right Data Structure**:
```java
// O(1) random access - use ArrayList
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

// Frequent insertions/deletions at ends - use LinkedList
List<Integer> deque = new LinkedList<>();

// Unique elements, fast lookup - use HashSet
Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

// Sorted unique elements - use TreeSet
Set<Integer> sorted = new TreeSet<>();

// Key-value storage - use HashMap
Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
```

**2. Avoid String Concatenation in Loops**:
```java
// ✗ Bad: Creates many String objects
String result = "";
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
    result += i;  // Creates new string each iteration
}

// ✓ Good: Single StringBuilder
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
    sb.append(i);
}
String result = sb.toString();
```

**3. Use Primitive Arrays when Possible**:
```java
// ✗ Less efficient
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

// ✓ More efficient for primitives
int[] array = new int[100];
```

**4. Cache Expensive Operations**:
```java
// ✗ Bad: Recalculates size each iteration
for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    System.out.println(list.get(i));
}

// ✓ Good: Cache size
int size = list.size();
for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
    System.out.println(list.get(i));
}
```

### Common Pitfalls and Solutions

**1. Null Pointer Exception**:
```java
// ✗ Risky
String str = null;
System.out.println(str.length());  // NullPointerException

// ✓ Safe
if (str != null) {
    System.out.println(str.length());
}

// ✓ Better (Java 8+)
Optional<String> optStr = Optional.ofNullable(str);
optStr.ifPresent(s -> System.out.println(s.length()));
```

**2. Modifying Collection During Iteration**:
```java
// ✗ ConcurrentModificationException
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
for (String item : list) {
    list.remove(item);  // Modifying while iterating
}

// ✓ Use Iterator
Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) {
    if (it.next().equals("remove")) {
        it.remove();  // Safe way
    }
}

// ✓ Create new list
list = list.stream()
    .filter(item -> !item.equals("remove"))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

**3. Comparing Objects**:
```java
// ✗ Wrong for Strings
String s1 = new String("Hello");
String s2 = new String("Hello");
if (s1 == s2) { }  // false (different objects)

// ✓ Correct
if (s1.equals(s2)) { }  // true (same value)
if (s1.equalsIgnoreCase(s2)) { }  // true (case-insensitive)
```

### Design Patterns

**Singleton Pattern** (single instance):
```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance;
    
    private Singleton() { }
    
    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}

// Or use eager initialization
public class EagerSingleton {
    private static final EagerSingleton instance = new EagerSingleton();
    
    private EagerSingleton() { }
    
    public static EagerSingleton getInstance() {
        return instance;
    }
}
```

**Factory Pattern** (create objects without specifying classes):
```java
public abstract class Animal {
    abstract void sound();
}

public class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Woof");
    }
}

public class AnimalFactory {
    public static Animal createAnimal(String type) {
        switch(type) {
            case "dog": return new Dog();
            case "cat": return new Cat();
            default: return null;
        }
    }
}
```

**Observer Pattern** (notify multiple objects of state changes):
```java
public interface Observer {
    void update(String message);
}

public class Subject {
    private List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();
    
    public void attach(Observer observer) {
        observers.add(observer);
    }
    
    public void notify(String message) {
        for (Observer observer : observers) {
            observer.update(message);
        }
    }
}
```

